    {
      "id": "01",
      "title": "EXPECT or ASSERT?",
      "question": "When should a check be EXPECT, and when ASSERT?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Google Test, question one. When should a check be EXPECT, and when should it be ASSERT? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "When <em>EXPECT</em>, and when <em>ASSERT</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "A test is a macro with a suite name and a test name. Assert equal is fatal: on failure, it returns from the current function. Use it when continuing makes no sense, like indexing past the end of a short row. Expect equal is non fatal: it records the failure and keeps going, so one run shows every broken field. Stream extra context into any check. A fixture class holds shared setup. Each test F gets a fresh fixture object, so tests can't leak state into each other.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Fatal, or <em>keep going</em>",
            "file": "parser_test.cpp",
            "tag": "gtest",
            "lang": "cpp",
            "size": 21,
            "width": 1180,
            "code": "#include <gtest/gtest.h>\n\nTEST(ParserTest, SplitsTwoFields) {\n    auto row = Split(\"a,b\", ',');\n    ASSERT_EQ(row.size(), 2u);         // fatal: stop before indexing past the end\n    EXPECT_EQ(row[0], \"a\");            // non-fatal: record, keep going\n    EXPECT_EQ(row[1], \"b\") << \"input was a,b\";\n}\n\nclass OrderBookTest : public ::testing::Test {\nprotected:\n    void SetUp() override { book_.Add({.id = 1, .px = 100, .qty = 10}); }\n    OrderBook book_;                   // a fresh fixture for every TEST_F\n};\n\nTEST_F(OrderBookTest, CancelEmptiesTheBook) {\n    EXPECT_TRUE(book_.Cancel(1));\n    EXPECT_TRUE(book_.Empty());\n}",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "macro",
                "lines": "3",
                "note": "TEST(Suite, Name)"
              },
              {
                "at": "returns",
                "lines": "5",
                "note": "ASSERT_*: return from this function"
              },
              {
                "at": "records",
                "lines": "6-7",
                "note": "EXPECT_*: record and continue"
              },
              {
                "at": "Stream",
                "lines": "7",
                "note": "<< adds context to the failure message"
              },
              {
                "at": "fixture",
                "lines": "10-13",
                "note": "Shared setup lives in a fixture"
              },
              {
                "at": "fresh",
                "lines": "16-18",
                "note": "TEST_F: a new fixture per test"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "Fixture lifecycle",
      "question": "Two TEST_Fs share a fixture. Do they share its state?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. Two test F tests use the same fixture. Do they share its state? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Two <em>TEST_F</em>s share a fixture. Do they share <em>state</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "No. For every test, Google Test constructs a new fixture object. Then it calls SetUp, runs the test body, calls TearDown, and destroys the object. The next test starts again from a brand new fixture, so order never matters. And because ASSERT only returns from the function it's in, TearDown still runs after a fatal failure. For expensive resources, like a database, a static SetUp test suite runs once for the whole suite, and that state is shared, so treat it as read only.",
          "props": {
            "title": "A fresh fixture, <em>every time</em>",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "id": "ctor",
                "label": "new fixture",
                "x": 200,
                "y": 330,
                "w": 230,
                "h": 64
              },
              {
                "id": "setup",
                "label": "SetUp()",
                "x": 530,
                "y": 330,
                "w": 210,
                "h": 64
              },
              {
                "id": "body",
                "label": "test body",
                "x": 860,
                "y": 330,
                "w": 210,
                "h": 64
              },
              {
                "id": "tear",
                "label": "TearDown()",
                "x": 1190,
                "y": 330,
                "w": 220,
                "h": 64
              },
              {
                "id": "dtor",
                "label": "delete fixture",
                "x": 1530,
                "y": 330,
                "w": 240,
                "h": 64
              },
              {
                "id": "next",
                "label": "next TEST_F:\nbrand-new fixture",
                "x": 530,
                "y": 610,
                "w": 300,
                "h": 86
              },
              {
                "id": "suite",
                "label": "SetUpTestSuite()\nonce per suite",
                "x": 1190,
                "y": 610,
                "w": 300,
                "h": 86,
                "hidden": true
              }
            ],
