    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Many inputs, many types",
      "question": "How do you run one test over many inputs, or many types?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. How do you run one test body over many inputs, or many types, without copying it? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "One test body, many <em>inputs</em> or <em>types</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "A value parameterized test derives from test with param. Test P reads get param, and instantiate test suite P supplies the values: each becomes its own test. Typed tests do the same for types: list the implementations, and type param is each one in turn. And a death test checks that code aborts with a message. It runs in a child process, and by convention its suite name ends in death test, so it runs first.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Parameterized, typed, <em>death</em>",
            "file": "params_test.cpp",
            "tag": "gtest",
            "lang": "cpp",
            "size": 20,
            "width": 1190,
            "code": "class IsPrimeTest : public ::testing::TestWithParam<int> {};\n\nTEST_P(IsPrimeTest, AcceptsPrimes) {\n    EXPECT_TRUE(IsPrime(GetParam())) << GetParam();\n}\nINSTANTIATE_TEST_SUITE_P(Small, IsPrimeTest, ::testing::Values(2, 3, 5, 7, 11));\n\ntemplate <typename T> class StackTest : public ::testing::Test {};\nusing Impls = ::testing::Types<VectorStack<int>, ListStack<int>>;\nTYPED_TEST_SUITE(StackTest, Impls);\n\nTYPED_TEST(StackTest, StartsEmpty) {\n    TypeParam s;                       // each implementation in turn\n    EXPECT_TRUE(s.empty());\n}\n\nTEST(DivideDeathTest, ZeroAborts) {\n    EXPECT_DEATH(Divide(1, 0), \"division by zero\");   // runs in a child process\n}",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "reads",
                "lines": "1-4",
                "note": "GetParam(): this run's value"
              },
              {
                "at": "supplies",
                "lines": "6",
                "note": "Each value → its own test"
              },
              {
                "at": "implementations",
                "lines": "8-10",
                "note": "One suite per type list"
              },
              {
                "at": "turn",
                "lines": "12-14",
                "note": "TypeParam is each type in turn"
              },
              {
                "at": "aborts",
                "lines": "17-18",
                "note": "Child process; *DeathTest suites run first"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "Run a subset with a filter: globs, and a minus sign to exclude. To chase a flaky test, repeat it a few hundred times, and shuffle the order to expose tests that depend on each other. Shuffle prints its seed, so you can replay a bad order. In CMake, link G test main, and discover tests, so every single test becomes its own C test entry.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Run it <em>well</em>",
            "file": "terminal",
            "tag": "shell",
            "lang": "shell",
            "size": 22,
            "width": 1180,
            "code": "# run a subset: Suite.Test globs, '-' excludes\n./tests --gtest_filter='OrderBook*:-*Slow*'\n\n# flaky? repeat it. order-dependent? shuffle (the seed is printed)\n./tests --gtest_repeat=200 --gtest_shuffle --gtest_break_on_failure\n\n# CMake: one CTest entry per TEST, discovered from the binary\n#   target_link_libraries(tests PRIVATE GTest::gtest_main)\n#   gtest_discover_tests(tests)\nctest --output-on-failure -j8",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "filter",
                "lines": "2",
                "note": "Suite.Test globs; '-' excludes"
              },
              {
                "at": "repeat",
                "lines": "5",
                "note": "Repeat to reproduce flakiness"
              },
              {
                "at": "seed",
                "lines": "5",
                "note": "Shuffle exposes order dependence"
              },
              {
                "at": "discover",
                "lines": "8-10",
                "note": "One CTest entry per TEST"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. ASSERT stops the current function; EXPECT keeps going. Every test F gets a fresh fixture. Parameterized and typed tests reuse one body. And repeat and shuffle flush out flaky tests.",
          "props": {
            "title": "GoogleTest, <em>in four lines</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "stops",
                "text": "ASSERT stops the function; EXPECT keeps going.",
                "code": "ASSERT_EQ · EXPECT_EQ"
              },
              {
                "at": "fresh",
                "text": "Each TEST_F gets a fresh fixture.",
                "code": "SetUp · TearDown"
              },
              {
                "at": "reuse",
                "text": "TEST_P and TYPED_TEST reuse one body.",
                "code": "Values · Types"
              },
              {
                "at": "flush",
                "text": "Repeat and shuffle flush out flaky tests.",
                "code": "--gtest_shuffle"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
